  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. The decision framework: when sea, when air</h2>
      <p>
        This is the page’s core. The choice is a cargo-fit and cash-flow problem, not a rate
        comparison. Score the shipment on <strong>volume/density</strong>, <strong>value
        density</strong>, <strong>urgency</strong>, <strong>shelf life</strong> and
        <strong>working capital</strong>, then run the two quotes; the hybrid plan resolves conflicts.
      </p>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'dimension', label: 'Dimension' },
          { key: 'chooseSea', label: 'Choose sea when...' },
          { key: 'chooseAir', label: 'Choose air when...' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={decisionMatrixRows}
        caption="The decision matrix is heuristic. The compliance row is the only HIGH-confidence line; the rest are planning patterns, not fixed rules."
      />
      <SourceList sources={decisionSources} title="Sources — decision framework context" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">Chargeable weight vs CBM: the arithmetic that decides the premium</h3>
      <p>
        Sea LCL bills by <strong>cubic metre</strong> and FCL by <strong>container</strong>; air bills
        by <strong>chargeable kilogram</strong> — the higher of actual and volumetric weight, where
        volumetric weight is <strong>L × W × H (cm) ÷ 6,000</strong>. Some carriers use ÷ 5,000, making
        the bill larger.
      </p>
      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
          { key: 'sea', label: 'Sea freight' },
          { key: 'air', label: 'Air freight' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={billingRows}
        caption="Sea scales by cube, air by chargeable weight. The 6,000-divisor rule is MEDIUM confidence; the patterns are LOW confidence."
      />
      <SourceList sources={chargeableSources} title="Sources — chargeable weight rule" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Secondary note — the FCL/LCL ~15 CBM break-even:</strong> within sea, FCL usually wins
        above roughly 15 CBM and LCL usually wins below it, because FCL is a flat box rate spread
        across your cube. Air sits alongside both when speed or value density outweighs the
        chargeable-weight bill. Read the full crossover in the
        <a href="/middleeast/fcl-vs-lcl/">FCL vs LCL guide</a>.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

